column47:
Use of 'stock version' in description of supported mobile browsers needs clarifying [1].

From discussions with Karel and Marek S.:
{quote}
I believe the point of using that term is that the internal android browser/ internal iOS browser was tested (and not e.g. Chrome or any other browser that can be used on Android/iOS). AFAIK neither Android nor iOS tells you any meaningful version number of the internal browser, so the only identifier one can use is "whatever internal browser is in Android 2.3, iOS 7", etc., which is what the "stock version in ..." was meant to convey.
{quote}
and
{quote}
Marek is right, for Android stock browser is basically a WebView widget defined by Android version. This is different starting with Android 4.4, where Chrome (30) is a foundation for WebView. Interesting article here: http://www.mobilexweb.com/blog/android-4-4-kitkat-browser-chrome-webview
As for iOS, stock browser is Safari, where the version is again bound to iOS version, same as for Android.
{quote}

[1] https://access.redhat.com/site/articles/112543

