column47:
Jackson2JsonpInterceptor does not render closing bracket

So
bq. foo({"foo":"bar"}
Is rendered instead of
bq. foo({"foo":"bar"})

The problem is in this method:
{code:java|title=Jackson2JsonpInterceptor.java}    @Override
    public void aroundWriteTo(WriterInterceptorContext context) throws IOException, WebApplicationException {
        String function = uri.getQueryParameters().getFirst(callbackQueryParameter);
        if (function != null && !function.trim().isEmpty() && !jsonpCompatibleMediaTypes.getPossible(context.getMediaType()).isEmpty()){
            OutputStreamWriter writer = new OutputStreamWriter(context.getOutputStream());

            writer.write(function + "(");
            writer.flush();
            context.proceed();
            writer.write(")");
            writer.flush();
        } else {
            context.proceed();
        }
    }{code}

context.proceed() call can close the underlying OutputStream and the bracket appended on the next line will be ignored

After calling the following code to add a facesmessage
   FacesContext.getCurrentInstance().addMessage
to show it in <h:messages /> component, if you send a non-ajax request using component 
   <h:commandButton id="nonAjaxAction" value="Add Message"
                                action="#{actionBean.addMessage}">
                        </h:commandButton><!-- actionBean is sessionScoped -->
the message is displayed on the page without issue. And if you navigate to a different portlet page, for example the built-in home page provided by JPP 6, and then navigate back to the page the facesmessage is gone. This is the expected behavior.

If you send an ajax request using component 
    <h:commandButton id="ajaxAction" value="Add Message (AJAX)"
                                action="#{actionBean.addAjaxMessage}">
                                <f:ajax render="@form" execute="@form" />
    </h:commandButton><!-- actionBean is sessionScoped -->
the message is displayed on the page as expected. However, if you navigate to a different page and then navigate back to this page the facesmessage remains on the page. This is not expected. 

The following code is called for a ActionRequest and/or RenderRequest but not for a ResourceRequest.

org.jboss.portletbridge.bridge.controller.Jsf20ControllerImpl.java:


 protected void restoreMessages(FacesContext facesContext, BridgeRequestScope scope) {
        if (facesContext.getExternalContext().getRequest() instanceof RenderRequest) {
            FacesMessageWrapper messageWrapper = (FacesMessageWrapper) scope.remove(FACES_MESSAGES_WRAPPER);
            if (null != messageWrapper) {
                for (String clientId : messageWrapper.getClientIds()) {
                    for (FacesMessage msg : messageWrapper.getMessages(clientId)) {
                        facesContext.addMessage(clientId, msg);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }


The FacesMessages are removed from BridgeRequestScope for portlet RenderRequest. Please note this line of code:
  FacesMessageWrapper messageWrapper = (FacesMessageWrapper) scope.remove(FACES_MESSAGES_WRAPPER);

So, a non-ajax request is interpreted as a ActionRequest. The FacesMessages are restored in the subsequent RenderRequest phase. In the meanwhile, the FacesMessages are removed from BridgeRequestScope. As a result, the next RenderRequest will not show the messages on the page. 

An ajax request, however, is interpreted as a ResourceRequest. This restoreMessages method is not even called for a ResourceRequest. Therefore, the FacesMessages are not cleaned from BridgeRequestScope. As a result, the next RenderRequest will still show the messages on the page.

I don't see an easy fix for this issue.

The difficulty is there's no way to clean the FacesMessages from BridgeRequestScope in a Resource phase. The BridgeRequestScope only makes sense for a RenderRequest. Here is the code to get the BridgeRequestScope:

protected BridgeRequestScope getBridgeRequestScope(BridgeContext bridgeContext) {
        BridgeRequestScope scope = null;
        if (Bridge.PortletPhase.RENDER_PHASE == bridgeContext.getPortletRequestPhase()) {
            String bridgeRequestScopeId = bridgeContext.getPortletRequest().getParameter(REQUEST_SCOPE_ID);
            if (null != bridgeRequestScopeId) {
                scope = bridgeContext.getBridgeRequestScopeManager().getRequestScopeById(bridgeContext, bridgeRequestScopeId);
            }
        }
        if (null == scope) {
            scope = bridgeContext.getBridgeScope();
        }
        // TODO Add something to ignore it if a setting is true? ie. http://jira.portletfaces.org/browse/BRIDGE-219
        return scope;
    }

The proposed fix is to not add the facesMessages to the scope. 
However, it seems that this approach will break portletbridge spec:
"Resource requests are an additional phase in the overall portlet render lifecyle.  Following a render, one or more resource requests are made to either acquire dependent resources to complete the rendition or update parts of the existing rendition.  As such, resource requests run within the same restored bridge managed request scope as its render request.   What is unique to resources, however, is they can be a POSTBACK.  I.e. they can be a Faces view (form) submit requesting a partial update and rendition and hence require the full Faces action lifecycle to be run.  So like the event processing described above, resource request handling also has to deal with saving changes to the restored scope so that subsequent render requests will have complete and updated state.  The details on managing this are describe below in section [5.2.7].".

The current plan is to provide an option from configuration so that users could enable it or not to not add the facesMessages to scope based on their own needs.

I have simple application called netflix.war and in portlet.xml of this application I have:
{code}
<init-param>
  <name>javax.portlet.faces.defaultViewId.view</name>
  <value>/pages/netflix.xhtml</value>
</init-param>
{code}

There is the issue that name of xhtml page "/pages/netflix.xhtml" contains contextPath string "/netflix" which is causing issues.


Steps to reproduce with my simple application (assuming you have GateIn with JBoss AS7 in /tmp/GateIn-JBoss7 ) :
{code}
git clone git://github.com/mposolda/netflix.git
cd netflix
git checkout origin/pbr-bug
mvn clean install
cp target/netflix.war /tmp/GateIn-JBoss7/standalone/deployments/
cd /tmp/GateIn-JBoss7/bin/
./standalone.sh
{code}

Then:
- login as root
- add NetflixPortlet to some page
- Fill name of some movie and click "Search" button
You will see this exception in server log:
{code}
Caused by: java.lang.NullPointerException
	at com.sun.faces.context.PartialViewContextImpl.createPartialResponseWriter(PartialViewContextImpl.java:441) [jsf-impl-2.1.11-jbossorg-3.jar:]
	at com.sun.faces.context.PartialViewContextImpl.access$300(PartialViewContextImpl.java:71) [jsf-impl-2.1.11-jbossorg-3.jar:]
	at com.sun.faces.context.PartialViewContextImpl$DelayedInitPartialResponseWriter.getWrapped(PartialViewContextImpl.java:582) [jsf-impl-2.1.11-jbossorg-3.jar:]
	at javax.faces.context.PartialResponseWriter.startDocument(PartialResponseWriter.java:115) [jboss-jsf-api_2.1_spec-2.0.4.Final.jar:2.0.4.Final]
	at com.sun.faces.context.AjaxExceptionHandlerImpl.handlePartialResponseError(AjaxExceptionHandlerImpl.java:199) [jsf-impl-2.1.11-jbossorg
{code}


From my investigation, the exception is caused by method BridgeContextImpl.getFacesViewIdFromPath(String path)
The input path is something like "/pages/netflix.jsf" but this code snippet:
{code}
        // Now remove up through the ContextPath
        String ctxPath = getPortletRequest().getContextPath();
        index = path.indexOf(ctxPath);
        if (index != -1) {
            path = path.substring(index + ctxPath.length());
        }
{code}
will cause that returned path and returned viewId is only string ".jsf" because ctxPath is "/netflix" which is contained in "/pages/netflix.jsf". This will later cause NPE when trigger JSF action via Search button

Liferay (in my case 6.0.6 with property request.shared.attributes=LIFERAY_SHARED_,PORTLET_BREADCRUMB_MAP,javax.portlet.markup.head.element) generates empty self-closing <script> tags

{code}
<script src="..." />
{code}

that mess the HTML. Browsers do do display such pages properly.

Hello,

In our high loaded portal we have many this errors in log:

00:10:51,062 SEVERE [javax.enterprise.resource.webcontainer.jsf.application] (http--192.168.1.12-8080-3) Error Rendering View[/portlets/actsdatabank/acts.xhtml]: java.lang.ClassCastException: org.jboss.portletbridge.context.flash.FlashHttpServletAdapter cannot be cast to javax.portlet.PortletResponse
        at org.jboss.portletbridge.context.PortletExternalContextImpl.getPortletResponse(PortletExternalContextImpl.java:186) [portletbridge-impl-3.1.0.Final.jar:]
        at org.jboss.portletbridge.context.PortletExternalContextImpl.encodeURL(PortletExternalContextImpl.java:532) [portletbridge-impl-3.1.0.Final.jar:]
        at org.jboss.portletbridge.context.PortletExternalContextImpl.encodeResourceURL(PortletExternalContextImpl.java:979) [portletbridge-impl-3.1.0.Final.jar:]
        at javax.faces.context.ExternalContextWrapper.encodeResourceURL(ExternalContextWrapper.java:132) [jboss-jsf-api_2.1_spec-2.0.1.Final.jar:2.0.1.Final]

IMHO I found a problem in PB code, but I don't know how to resolve this:
look to org.jboss.portletbridge.context.AbstractExternalContext.
{code:title=org.jboss.portletbridge.context.AbstractExternalContext|borderStyle=solid}
    public Object getResponse() {
        if (isBridgeFlashServletResponse()) {
            return new FlashHttpServletAdapter((PortletResponse)response);
        }
        return this.response;
    }

    protected boolean isBridgeFlashServletResponse() {
        return ((null != portletFlash) && portletFlash.isServletResponse());
    }
{code}

Property portletFlash initialized through
PortletFlash.getFlash(ExternalContext externalContext, boolean create);
if you look in PortletFlash.getFlash then you'll see:
{code:title=org.jboss.portletbridge.context.flash.PortletFlash|borderStyle=solid}
    public static PortletFlash getFlash(ExternalContext externalContext, boolean create) {
        Map<String, Object> appMap = externalContext.getApplicationMap();
        PortletFlash flash = (PortletFlash) appMap.get(FLASH_ATTRIBUTE_NAME);
        if (null == flash && create) {
            synchronized (externalContext.getContext()) {
                if (null == (flash = (PortletFlash) appMap.get(FLASH_ATTRIBUTE_NAME))) {
                    flash = new PortletFlash(externalContext);
                    appMap.put(FLASH_ATTRIBUTE_NAME, flash);
                }
            }
        return flash;
    }
{code}

So all threads work with one instance PortletFlash from appMap, and when one thread calls org.jboss.portletbridge.context.flash.PortletFlash#doPostPhaseActions
or org.jboss.portletbridge.context.flash.PortletFlash#doLastPhaseActions
or org.jboss.portletbridge.context.flash.PortletFlash#put
other threads get true in org.jboss.portletbridge.context.AbstractExternalContext#isBridgeFlashServletResponse.

Thank you.
Alex.

