column47:
Relate to https://jira.exoplatform.org/browse/CS-5632

Scenario: Having a 2-nodes cluster setup for PLF 3.5.2 tomcat
Database: MySQL
Node 1 ../CLUSTER/NODE1 (port 8080)
Node 2 ../CLUSTER/NODE2 (port 8090)
Data   ../CLUSTER/DATA

Clien code:
{code}
List<CalendarEvent> events = new ArrayList<CalendarEvent>();
    eventQuery.setCalendarPath(calendarHome.getPath());
    QueryManager qm = calendarHome.getSession().getWorkspace().getQueryManager();
    Query query = qm.createQuery(eventQuery.getQueryStatement(), eventQuery.getQueryType());
    QueryResult result = query.execute();
    NodeIterator it = result.getNodes();
{code}

Result: QueryResult include 2 duplicate nodes

ScoreNode1: [e0b12b33c0a8016d01812b4fce7e5902(335)]
ScoreNode2: [e0b12b33c0a8016d01812b4fce7e5902(1679)]
2 these ScoreNodes have similar UUID, only DOCIDs are different

In JCR code:
{code}
org.exoplatform.services.jcr.impl.core.query.lucene.SortedLuceneQueryHits.getHits(){
      TopFieldDocCollector collector = new TopFieldDocCollector(reader, sort, numHits);
      searcher.search(query, collector);
      this.size = collector.getTotalHits();
      ScoreDoc[] docs = collector.topDocs().scoreDocs;
      for (int i = scoreNodes.size(); i < docs.length; i++)
      {
         String uuid = reader.document(docs[i].doc).get(FieldNames.UUID);
         //NodeId id = new NodeId(UUID.fromString(uuid));
         scoreNodes.add(new ScoreNode(uuid, docs[i].score, docs[i].doc));
      }
      log.debug("getHits() {}/{}", new Integer(scoreNodes.size()), new Integer(numHits));
      // double hits for next round
      numHits *= 2;
}
{code}

It's seem a bug from Lucene, and ScoreNodes are added to scoreNodes without checking duplicate,
=> bug in https://jira.exoplatform.org/browse/CS-5632

It appears that the changes log are not optimized which has for consequences to make more db changes than what is really needed for example:
{code}
node.setProperty("a", "x");
node.setProperty("a", "y");
node.setProperty("a", "z");
{code}
This is seen as 3 updates instead of only the last one
Another example:
{code}
node.setProperty("a", "x");
node.setProperty("a", null);
node.setProperty("a", "z");
{code}
This is seen as an update, a delete and a add but it could be seen as an update (the last one)
Another example:
{code}
Node node = root.addNode("xxx");
...
node.remove();
...
session.save();
{code}
In this case we add and remove the node in the same tx, we should simply remove it and its children from the changes log.

So the goal of this task is to optimize the changes log size in order to ensure that we will apply to the db the minimum amount of operations.

column280:
07/Jun/12 5:01 AM;nfilotto;This sound much better but there are 2 use cases that should be improved a little bit more:

Use case #1 :
   setProperty("x", "a");
   setProperty("x", "b");
   setProperty("x", "c");
 Expected persistent changes :
   setProperty("x", "c"); ADDED

Use case #2:
   setProperty("x", "a");
   save();
   setProperty("x", null);
   setProperty("x", "c");
Expected persistent changes :
   setProperty("x", "c"); UPDATED

Finally I would like to prevent updating a property with the exact same value as the current one, the idea would be to check into the item pool and the JCR cache if the property exists if not we assume that the value is new so we apply the change if it exists we compare the values if they are equal we skip the change otherwise we apply it.

One way to define the use case will be to use a first session that will set a property "x" to "a" then save, a second session will get the same node and will try to set the same value to "x", no change is expected.

