column277:
16/Nov/11 5:37 AM;tolusha;Stored procedure
{code}
CREATE PROCEDURE PGetPath
(
   @idItem VARCHAR(512),
   @currentPath VARCHAR(16384) = '' output
)
as
begin
   declare @currentID VARCHAR(96)
   declare @currentIndex INT
   declare @currentName VARCHAR(512)

   set @currentID=@idItem 
   while (@currentID != '')
   begin
     SELECT @currentName=NAME, @currentID=PARENT_ID, @currentIndex=I_INDEX from JCR_SITEM where ID=@currentID
     SET @currentPath = @currentName || ':' || LTRIM(RTRIM(STR(@currentIndex))) || @currentPath
   end
end
{code}

column47:
Actual JCR codebase doesn't use delayed (lazy) indexing for big amounts of data. But LazyTextExtractorField class indexing queue are present in code and partially involved in the process of indexing. This has two possible negative effects:
* memory consumption, that later freed, but actually GC'ed much later (often reported by Cloud Team, and partially fixed as workaround in https://issues.jboss.org/browse/EXOJCR-1337 );
* possibly can have negative effect on the process of Document's delivery and indexing on coordinator side, because it can have StringReader that is not serializeable itself (additional investigation required).

So in general this issues proposes to finish partial work done in https://issues.jboss.org/browse/EXOJCR-1337 :
* Get rid of pending documents indexing : a dead no-working feature partially present in code. This feature is not compatible with clustering:
** remove LazyTextExtractorFiled;
** remove indexing queues present on the level of AbstractIndex class;
** remove TextExtractorReader, that is in the codebase, but not used, cause feature is not implemented and partially removed already.

column278:
21/Oct/11 3:19 AM;tolusha;There are 3 choices of JCR Locks migration from 1.12.x to 1.14.x
I. When new Shareable Cache feature is not going to be used and all locks should be kept after migration.
* Ensure that the same lock tables used in configuration;
* Start the server 

II. When new Shareable Cache feature is not going to be used and all locks should be removed after migration.
* Ensure that the same lock tables used in configuration;
* Start the sever WITH system property -Dorg.exoplatform.jcr.locks.force.remove=true ;
* Stop the server;
* Start the server (WITHOUT system property -Dorg.exoplatform.jcr.locks.force.remove);

III. When new Shareable Cache feature will be used (in this case all locks are removed after migration).
* Start the sever WITH system property -Dorg.exoplatform.jcr.locks.force.remove=true ;
* Stop the server;
* Start the server (WITHOUT system property -Dorg.exoplatform.jcr.locks.force.remove);
* (Not mandatory) manually remove old tables for lock;



>> So for the second issue, you will be able to remove old locks created by JCR 1.12 once upgraded to JCR 1.14 (EPP 5.2) ? 
yes

>> In this case, if we use shareable and same table for locks customers will still have old N tables in their database.  
yes

>> But if I understand it correctly, these tables will be empty then (because locks will be removed) and so it should not be a problem for migration ? 
This tables will not be empty, just remain after migration and it is not a problem. It is possible to remove old tables for locks if need.

>> Especially I wanted to know if customers will be able to upgrade to shareable without doing any manual steps with their DB (aka deleting old lock tables).
yes

