column280:
04/Aug/11 12:54 PM;nfilotto;If you compare the perfs results on JCR 1.12.x and 1.14.x for this particular test, you will notice that they are really similar http://tests.exoplatform.org/JCR/1.12.10-GA/rev.4553/japex-reports/RandomReadNtFileWithMetadataTest/oracle/1/1.1/1.1.2/index.html and http://tests.exoplatform.org/JCR/1.14.0-GA/rev.4559/japex-reports/RandomReadNtFileWithMetadataTest/oracle/1/1.1/1.1.2/index.html

Actually the perf improvement was due to the following change:
Before the change
{code}
PrivilegedFileHelper.mkdirs(tfile.getParentFile());
{code}

After the change (which is actually the same code as JCR 1.12 and 1.10)
{code}
mkdirs(tfile.getParentFile()); // make dirs on path
{code}

And if we compare the code of those methods, we realize that the code of PrivilegedFileHelper.mkdirs doesn't do in a privileged block exactly what mkdirs(tfile.getParentFile()) does as expected, indeed the code of PrivilegedFileHelper.mkdirs is:

{code}
   public static boolean mkdirs(final File file)
   {
      PrivilegedAction<Boolean> action = new PrivilegedAction<Boolean>()
      {
         public Boolean run()
         {
            return file.mkdirs();
         }
      };
      return SecurityHelper.doPrivilegedAction(action);
   }
{code}

And the code of TreeFileIOChannel.mkdirs is
{code}
   static private boolean mkdirs(final File dir)
   {
      // TODO issue on JIRA syncronized method removed and semaphore use
      try
      {
         mkdirsLock.acquire();
         return dir.mkdirs();
      }
      catch (InterruptedException e)
      {
         // chLog.error("mkdirs error on " + dir.getAbsolutePath() + ". " + e, e);
         // return false;
         throw new IllegalStateException("mkdirs error on " + dir.getAbsolutePath() + " due to " + e, e);
      }
      finally
      {
         mkdirsLock.release();
      }
   }
{code}

So thanks to this perf regression, we could find a very old perf killer that exists at least since JCR 1.10.x. So the next step will be to try to find the reason why we added this ugly lock and if it is really needed to use a smarter mechanism based on a ConcurrentMap<String, Lock> where the String would be the path.

