max_connections=339
innodb_thread_concurrency=8
innodb_lock_wait_timeout - default (50 s)

with different data source config:
<property name="maxActive" value="100" />
  CQ - first exception become later than 30 min, nonCQ - never; 
<property name="maxActive" value="200" />
  CQ - first exception become fast (1-2 min), nonCQ - after 10 min; 
<property name="maxActive" value="300" />
  CQ - first exception become fast (1-2 min), nonCQ - after 4-5 min; 

Both <property name="maxActive" value="300" /> and innodb_lock_wait_timeout=120
  CQ - first exception become later than 30 min; 

So, two paramters affects on stable work with MySQL InnoDB: 
- max active connections in jcr data source configuration; 
- innodb_lock_wait_timeout in MySQL server configuration;

column277:
18/May/10 10:08 AM;nzamosenchuk;As it turned out, Near Real-Time search feature in lucene is implemented by flushing the content of writer to the Directory.
This feature is obviously slower than RAM operations in volatile index.

{code}
  public IndexReader getReader(int termInfosIndexDivisor) throws IOException {

    ensureOpen();

    if (infoStream != null) {
      message("flush at getReader");
    }

    // Do this up front before flushing so that the readers
    // obtained during this flush are pooled, the first time
    // this method is called:
    poolReaders = true;

    flush(true, true, false);

    // Prevent segmentInfos from changing while opening the
    // reader; in theory we could do similar retry logic,
    // just like we do when loading segments_N
    synchronized(this) {
      applyDeletes();
      final IndexReader r = new ReadOnlyDirectoryReader(this, segmentInfos, termInfosIndexDivisor, codecs);
      if (infoStream != null) {
        message("return reader version=" + r.getVersion() + " reader=" + r);
      }
      return r;
    }
  }
{code}

column284:
02/Jun/10 4:52 AM;nzamosenchuk;
What is known up to now:

* autoCommit feature is deprecated in 2.9+ and should be controlled out of Lucene. So we can manage commit operation in the same way as before, providing the same "commit on idle", "commit when buffer filled" features.
* There is an optimize operation, that re-builds index, making search faster. This procedure is / should be launched only manually and periodically;
* failover facilities can be provided using the same RedoLog-based mechanism. Having control over the commit, we can easily manage and cleanup RedoLog in time;

So SingleIndex with NRT (near real time) search can serve as an indexing subsystem in JCR replacing current MultiIndex implementation. Making it as production ready system needs some time to implement missing in POC features and have reasonable set of tests.

Benefits:
* Much simpler code, much easy to support;

Disadvantages:
* Time to implement/test;
* Not tested in production, as existing impl.

Under investigation:
* Search should be a bit faster with larger indexes.
* Possibly this implementation can give performance increase in concurrent environment, when multiple threads are writing and/or reading.

Note:
* is not directly affiliated with clustered implementation.

